course: history
lesson: 19
title: The Aztecs and the Inca
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: About 700 years ago a wandering people, the Aztecs or Mexica, settled in the Valley of Mexico. Legend says they built their city where they saw an eagle on a cactus, on an island in a lake; that eagle is on Mexico's flag today.
explain: Their capital, Tenochtitlan, had causeways, canals, floating gardens and a huge market, and was bigger than most European cities of the time. The Aztecs believed the sun needed offerings and sacrificed people, often war captives, so their neighbours feared and hated them.
explain: Far to the south, the Inca ruled along the Andes mountains, the largest empire in the Americas, joined by 40,000 kilometres of roads and rope bridges. They had no writing as we know it, but kept records with knotted strings called quipu.
explain: In 1519 Hernán Cortés arrived with a few hundred Spaniards, and in 1532 Francisco Pizarro reached the Inca. They did not win alone, like a small team in a film beating a giant: tens of thousands of native allies and smallpox did most of it.
fact: The Aztecs, or Mexica, were a wandering people who settled in the Valley of Mexico about 700 years ago and in about 200 years built one of the largest empires in the Americas.
fact: Legend says their god told them to build their city where they saw an eagle on a cactus, and the eagle on the cactus is on Mexico's flag today.
fact: Tenochtitlan, the Aztec capital, was built on a lake with causeways, canals, floating gardens and a huge market, and it was bigger than most European cities of the time; Mexico City stands on its ruins.
fact: The Aztecs believed the sun needed offerings to keep moving and made human sacrifices, often of war captives, and neighbouring peoples feared and hated them for this and for their heavy tribute, the goods conquered peoples had to hand over every year.
fact: In 1519 Hernán Cortés arrived with a few hundred men, and with tens of thousands of native allies and smallpox spreading he conquered Tenochtitlan in 1521.
fact: Mexico then became a Spanish colony for 300 years.
fact: About 500 years ago the Inca ruled a huge empire along the Andes mountains, from today's Ecuador to Chile, the largest empire in the Americas.
fact: The Inca built about 40,000 kilometres of roads with rope bridges over gorges, and relay runners carried messages very fast.
fact: The Inca kept records with quipu, strings with knots of different colours and positions, to count people, taxes and goods.
fact: Machu Picchu is an Inca town high on a mountain ridge, built of stones fitted so tightly that a knife blade cannot slip between them; the Spanish never found it, and it became world famous in 1911, though local people always knew it.
fact: Potatoes first came from the Andes and were later taken to Europe; the Andes peoples grew thousands of kinds of potatoes, freeze-dried them in the mountain cold, and kept llamas and alpacas for wool and carrying loads; the Inca had no horses and no carts with wheels, so people and llamas carried everything along the roads.
fact: In 1532 Francisco Pizarro captured the Inca emperor Atahualpa, who filled a room with gold to buy his freedom, but the Spaniards killed him anyway.
fact: A civil war and smallpox had already weakened the Inca, and Spain took over their empire.
exercise: About when did the Aztecs settle in the Valley of Mexico? | About 700 years ago | About 7,000 years ago | About 70 years ago, in 1950 | About 70,000 years ago
exercise: What sign told the Aztecs where to build their city? | An eagle on a cactus | A wolf feeding twins | A snake in a river | A falling star
exercise: Where can you see the eagle on the cactus today? | On Mexico's flag | On the US dollar bill | On an old Roman coin | On the Inca quipu
exercise: Where was Tenochtitlan built? | On a lake | On top of a volcano | In the desert | Under the sea
exercise: Which city stands on the ruins of Tenochtitlan? | Mexico City | Lima | Madrid | Cusco
exercise: Why did the Aztecs make sacrifices? | They believed the sun needed offerings to keep moving | They wanted to scare the Spanish away from Mexico for ever | It was a sport like football | Their king liked shows
exercise: Why did many neighbours hate the Aztecs? | For the sacrifices and the heavy tribute | Because the Aztecs had no army and could not fight back | Because the Aztecs were too poor | Because they spoke Spanish
exercise: Who conquered Tenochtitlan in 1521? | Hernán Cortés with native allies | Francisco Pizarro alone, with no help at all | The Inca army | The Vikings
exercise: For how long was Mexico a Spanish colony? | About 300 years | About 30 years | About 3,000 years | About 3 years
exercise: Along which mountains did the Inca rule? | The Andes | The Alps in Europe | The Himalayas | The Rockies
exercise: How long were the Inca roads in total? | About 40,000 kilometres | About 400 kilometres | About 4 kilometres | About 4 million kilometres
exercise: How did the Inca cross deep gorges? | With rope bridges | With iron railway bridges | By flying | By swimming
exercise: What was a quipu? | Knotted strings for keeping records | A kind of mountain potato | A rope bridge made of grass | A gold crown worn by the emperor
exercise: What is special about the stones of Machu Picchu? | They fit so tightly a knife cannot slip between them | They are glued together with melted chocolate and tree sap | They are made of gold | They float on water
exercise: Which animals did the Andes peoples keep for wool and loads? | Llamas and alpacas | Camels and horses | Elephants and oxen | Bison and deer
exercise: How did the Inca keep potatoes for a long time? | They freeze-dried them in the mountain cold | They kept them cold in fridges | They salted them in the sea and buried them in sand for years | They never kept them
exercise: Which Inca emperor did Pizarro capture? | Atahualpa | Montezuma | Pachacuti | Huayna Capac
test: a film shows a few hundred spaniards beating the whole aztec empire alone. true? | No, tens of thousands of native allies and disease did most of it | Yes, alone with swords | Yes, because the Aztecs had no army | No, the Inca beat them
test: Why were native peoples willing to help Cortés against the Aztecs? | They hated the Aztecs' sacrifices and tribute | They were paid in chocolate and gold | They wanted to move to Spain | They thought Cortés was their king and had come home at last
test: The Inca had no writing as we know it. How did they run a huge empire? | With quipu records, roads and fast relay runners | They did not run it at all; every town ruled itself | With letters on paper | With telephones on poles
test: If the Inca had had horses and wheels, what might have changed most? | Travel and carrying on their roads | The shape and size of Machu Picchu | The colour of the llamas | Nothing at all
test: bro says some american found machu picchu in 1911 and nobody knew it before. right? | No, local people always knew it; 1911 made it world famous | Yes, nobody had ever seen it | Yes, it was under the sea | No, the Spanish found it first
test: Why did Machu Picchu survive so well? | The Spanish never found it | It was made of plastic | The Spanish rebuilt it stone by stone | It is hidden deep underground
test: Atahualpa filled a room with gold to go free. What happened? | The Spaniards killed him anyway | He was freed and went home to rule again | He became king of Spain | He sailed to Europe
test: Why was the Inca Empire easier to take over in 1532? | A civil war and smallpox had already weakened it | It had no people left, because they all moved away | Its roads led to Spain | The Inca invited Pizarro to rule
test: Without the Andes, what might many European dinners lack today? | Potatoes | Bread | Cheese | Fish
test: A game puts the Aztecs and the Inca right next to each other. What is wrong? | The Aztecs were in Mexico, the Inca far south in the Andes | Nothing, they were close neighbours and traded every week | They were both in Spain | They were in Africa
test: Tenochtitlan was bigger than most European cities then. What does that tell a fair historian? | The Americas had great cities as rich as Europe's | Europe had no cities at all at that time | It was built by Europeans who sailed there earlier | The number must be wrong
exam: Identify the Spanish leader who in 1521 subdued Tenochtitlan with the assistance of numerous indigenous allies. | Hernán Cortés | Francisco Pizarro | Christopher Columbus | Ferdinand Magellan
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the quipu of the Inca? | Cords bearing knots by which records were kept | Bridges of rope across gorges | A variety of freeze-dried potato | The crown of the emperor
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the fall of the Aztec Empire. | Indigenous allies and epidemic disease contributed most to the Spanish victory | A few hundred Spaniards prevailed without assistance of any kind whatsoever | The Inca Empire conquered the Aztecs | The Aztecs surrendered voluntarily to Rome
exam: Ascertain the fate of the Inca emperor Atahualpa subsequent to the payment of his ransom in gold. | He was put to death by the Spaniards notwithstanding | He was released and restored to his throne | He was conveyed to Spain as a nobleman | He escaped to Machu Picchu
